All the agreed findings are applied, the corpus has been regenerated, and the focused JVM test passes: 14 tests, 2705 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors. `--check` reports the JSON matches the generator output byte for byte. Nothing is staged or committed.

## Per finding
- **qwen P2-1 (metadata map with its own metadata): agree.** Both the generator's encode side and its decode checker now raise `malformed-frame` instead of crashing. I added the decode fixture `meta/decode-meta-with-own-meta` and an encode twin, `meta/meta-with-own-meta`. The ruling is recorded under A3 and A10.
- **qwen P3-1 / deepseek P3-1 (required refusals and categories): agree.** The test now requires all 16 refusal classes and checks that set equals the corpus header's `refusal-classes`. `unsupported-values` is now a required category in both the test and the generator.
- **qwen P3-2 (integer-width duplicate): agree.** Added `coll/map-integer-width-duplicate`, refused as `duplicate-key`.
- **qwen P3-3 / deepseek P3-2 (sorted collection with metadata): agree.** Added `meta/sorted-map-b-a-doc`, which has the same bytes as `meta/map-a-b-doc`. Also added `meta/sorted-set-reader-meta`, which has the same bytes as the plain set `{1 2 3}`.
- **deepseek P3-3 (float versus decimal collapse): agree.** Added `coll/set-float-decimal-collapse` and its decode twin `mal/collapse-float-decimal-set`. I expect it to be constructible on the JVM only, since Clojure's `=` treats 1.0 and 1M as unequal there; I did not run this.
- **qwen P3-4 (N/A collapse cases): agree.** The README now has a JVM/Node/Dart table saying which encode-side collapse and duplicate refusals can actually be built on each host. It is labelled as a review estimate, not something I ran.
- **qwen P3-5 (symbol metadata wrapping): agree.** A4 now covers symbols as well as lists.
- **qwen P3-6 (symbol slash crossings): agree.** Added `id/symbol-ns-a-slash-b-name-c` and `id/symbol-ns-a-name-b-slash-c`. I also added `id/symbol-empty-name`, the optional addition under the A8 ruling.
- **qwen P3-7 (frame-name matching): agree.** The test's `includes-bytes?` now matches frame names only at byte boundaries (even offsets).
- **A9 ruling (validate before strip): applied.**
  - The encoder now checks the values under stripped keys before dropping them.
  - Encode fixture `meta/surrogate-under-stripped-line` is refused as `unpaired-surrogate`.
  - On the wire a surrogate can only appear as invalid UTF-8, so the decode fixture `meta/decode-invalid-utf8-under-line` is refused as `invalid-utf8`. It deliberately has two defects, to pin that validation comes before the canonical check. The README records it as the one exception to the single-defect rule.
- **A4, A5, A6, A8, A11 rulings: applied** to the ambiguity text. All the rulings are also gathered in a new "Rulings" section so a J1 implementer can read them in one place.
- **deepseek P3-4 (A8 note): agree.** Covered by the A8 ruling text.
- **qwen P3-8 (commit and wire `--check` into CI): declined.** Committing is not allowed for me, and CI files are outside the file box.
- **deepseek P2-2 (confirm A4 against Boring): declined for J0.** It needs Boring. The README now says J1 must confirm Boring can emit this shape or build the frame explicitly.
- **deepseek P2-3 (possible Dart loader problems): declined, no change.**
  - Bare `List`/`Map` already work without an import in `dao.stream.transit.cljd`, which requires only `dart:convert`.
  - `Uint8List.fromList` over Clojure data follows the pattern `file_test.cljc` already uses.
  - Both points stay unverified until the CLJD lane runs.

